    "description": "<p>Assume the flagged polygonal-transform and exact-factoring interfaces specified in the paper. We construct a polynomial-time uniform quantum circuit family over one fixed finite gate set that computes discrete logarithms in prime fields with probability one and polynomial worst-case resources. For prime-order inputs, each verified Fourier-bin label has probability at least 1/(81q). Given its verified logarithm, that probability is computable exactly by a bounded number of lattice-point counts in dimension at most thirteen. Dyadic completion produces success probability exactly one quarter, followed by one exact amplification step. All sampled probabilities are dyadic; no input-dependent rotation or postselection is used.</p>",
    "references": [
      {
        "reference": "A. Barvinok and J. E. Pommersheim, An algorithmic theory of lattice points in polyhedra, in L. J. Billera, A. Björner, C. Greene, R. Simion, and R. P. Stanley (eds.), New Perspectives in Algebraic Combinatorics, MSRI Publications 38, Cambridge University Press, 1999, 91–147. https://library.slmath.org/books/Book38/files/barvinok.pdf."
      },
      {
        "reference": "G. Brassard, P. Høyer, M. Mosca, and A. Tapp, Quantum amplitude amplification and estimation, Contemporary Mathematics 305 (2002), 53–74. arXiv:quant-ph/0005055."
      },
      {
        "reference": "J.-Y. Cai and B. Young, Quantum Algorithms for Discrete Log Require Precise Rotations, arXiv:2412.17269, 2024."
      },
      {
        "reference": "M. Mosca and C. Zalka, Exact quantum Fourier transforms and discrete logarithm algorithms, International Journal of Quantum Information 2 (2004), 91–100. arXiv:quant-ph/0301093."
      },
      {
        "reference": "OpenAI, Exact quantum factoring over a fixed finite gate set, September 25, 2026. Repository revision fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb. Pinned manuscript source."
      }
    ]
